1
00:00:17,931 --> 00:00:26,275
Mate, you are often referring that we should go back to the sūtas, actually, what is written in the sūtas.

2
00:00:26,615 --> 00:00:38,020
Is that kind of... is that your personal approach, or there is, how should I say, a school, should I call it, within saṅgha, within big

3
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:39,040
Right.

4
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:42,441
Well, I think it's just a question of label, you know.

5
00:00:42,601 --> 00:00:51,142
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm sure there are a few more monks that would agree with me in terms of going back to the suttas and not trusting the later Abhidhamma in commentaries.

6
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,483
[SPEAKER_00]: But I wouldn't say there is a school of us that we got together and decided that.

7
00:01:09,703 --> 00:01:16,168
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like just individuals on the base of their practice, so that that makes more sense, and it's least risky.

8
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:21,553
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, so that, like, you know, the latest stuff, it's often contradicting what the Buddha said.

9
00:01:22,173 --> 00:01:23,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And therefore, you can't trust it.

10
00:01:25,816 --> 00:01:26,497
In regard...

11
00:01:27,305 --> 00:01:37,475
To what is said now, can you give us some advice on how to do metta bhāvanā, how to do metta meditation?

12
00:01:37,915 --> 00:01:49,907
Because it seems to me that it is very related, what you mentioned, to accept everything as it is and, you know, how to say to

13
00:01:52,281 --> 00:01:58,687
to regard to everything what is happening to us with a kind of positive attitude.

14
00:01:59,508 --> 00:02:13,982
[SPEAKER_03]: However, during usual description of metta meditation, very often it's said that we

15
00:02:15,847 --> 00:02:26,494
[SPEAKER_03]: We're supposed to develop some kind of love or positive emotions towards, you know, our surroundings and everything what is happening.

16
00:02:26,934 --> 00:02:37,961
[SPEAKER_03]: But it seems to me that it should be more like you mentioned before, just to be open and to accept everything what is happening.

17
00:02:38,021 --> 00:02:43,405
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I would really appreciate if you could say a few words about the Metta meditation.

18
00:02:44,268 --> 00:02:58,697
[SPEAKER_03]: And especially, I'm interested, would you recommend to do, like, all four stages, or all four kinds, like metta, mudita, etc.?

19
00:02:58,677 --> 00:02:58,697
?

20
00:03:00,442 --> 00:03:06,264
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

21
00:03:07,544 --> 00:03:14,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, that's what you described as commonly... metta, as commonly taught, is not metta, really.

22
00:03:14,306 --> 00:03:17,167
[SPEAKER_00]: You read the suttas, there is no spreading of love and so on.

23
00:03:17,927 --> 00:03:19,288
[SPEAKER_00]: It's spreading of non-ill-will.

24
00:03:19,508 --> 00:03:20,728
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's no spreading either.

25
00:03:20,908 --> 00:03:26,430
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like expanding your mind, so to include everything else, so there is no basis for ill-will.

26
00:03:28,022 --> 00:03:35,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the mind is expanded in a sense that it cannot resist anything, good or bad, there is no basis for hatred to arise.

27
00:03:36,568 --> 00:03:37,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

28
00:03:37,828 --> 00:03:44,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the necessary basis for manifestation of any particular hatred is resistance, is refusal to accept it.

29
00:03:45,898 --> 00:03:58,722
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind is so expanded that it cannot resist anything anymore, it cannot resist this body, other bodies, in any direction of the world, then there is no basis for hatred and that's the pure metta, non-ill-will.

30
00:03:59,942 --> 00:04:03,843
[SPEAKER_00]: As I say often, opposite... love is not opposite of ill-will.

31
00:04:04,823 --> 00:04:06,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Love is opposite of non-love.

32
00:04:07,784 --> 00:04:10,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will is opposite of non-ill-will.

33
00:04:12,423 --> 00:04:16,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, if you practice metta, you will abandon both love and ill-will.

34
00:04:18,327 --> 00:04:21,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And as for the four stages, they're not four stages.

35
00:04:21,148 --> 00:04:25,991
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one practice that you can take on, like, higher levels.

36
00:04:26,291 --> 00:04:27,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are four of them.

37
00:04:27,772 --> 00:04:30,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start developing the first metta.

38
00:04:31,674 --> 00:04:33,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that, you will develop second.

39
00:04:33,496 --> 00:04:34,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that, you will develop third.

40
00:04:34,956 --> 00:04:36,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that, you can develop fourth.

41
00:04:36,377 --> 00:04:37,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like jhānas.

42
00:04:37,864 --> 00:04:45,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do one, two, three, four, but it's within establishing yourself in first jhāna, you then get to develop the second one.

43
00:04:47,067 --> 00:04:51,909
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within developing metta, you then get to develop karuṇa, upeka, eventually, in the end.

44
00:04:52,029 --> 00:04:53,170
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a matter of choice.

45
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:56,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, let me develop upeka and equanimity now.

46
00:04:56,631 --> 00:05:00,092
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible, because the mind is affected by your will and everything else.

47
00:05:02,453 --> 00:05:06,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how, if you want metta to do it in non-abstract sense,

48
00:05:07,871 --> 00:05:10,253
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to stop resisting yourself here.

49
00:05:10,674 --> 00:05:16,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings that you have, perceptions that you have, stop acting towards pleasant ones and stop resisting the uncomfortable ones.

50
00:05:17,701 --> 00:05:20,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's always why every metta starts with yourself.

51
00:05:21,525 --> 00:05:26,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by spreading love to yourself, but by developing non-resistance in regard to yourself.

52
00:05:27,486 --> 00:05:39,730
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't resist these five senses, if you don't act out of these five senses, you cannot resist or act out towards anything that comes on account of them, that is second to them, which is other people in the world.

53
00:05:44,212 --> 00:05:51,134
[SPEAKER_03]: Pante, what do you think about this vipassana which is taught by Goenka group?

54
00:05:51,814 --> 00:05:55,336
[SPEAKER_03]: What would be your comment on this practice?

55
00:05:55,696 --> 00:05:56,276
I'm interested.

56
00:05:58,141 --> 00:06:13,600
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a... it's a wrong... a wrong direction of the effort, because it's based... it's a practice that's based on observation of things, observation of your senses, observation of sensations, which are basically bodily touches, internal or external.

57
00:06:15,512 --> 00:06:30,241
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not based on understanding of the context, understanding of your intentions, understanding of the state of mind, because you expect just things to be shown to you through observation, not through effort to understand.

58
00:06:31,082 --> 00:06:34,364
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's ultimately a different direction from where the dhamma is.

59
00:06:36,445 --> 00:06:45,087
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why when Buddha said, when he described, for example, walking meditation, he didn't say you walk by feeling the soles of your feet and knees bending and so on.

60
00:06:45,728 --> 00:06:49,869
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, walking up and down, I purify my mind from unwholesome states.

61
00:06:50,349 --> 00:06:51,249
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the instruction.

62
00:06:52,169 --> 00:06:53,390
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the unwholesome state?

63
00:06:53,470 --> 00:07:02,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, lust is unwholesome state, greed is unwholesome state, jealousy is unwholesome state, envy, ill will, irritation, hatred, all of those are unwholesome states.

64
00:07:03,138 --> 00:07:04,800
[SPEAKER_00]: But what vipassana does on account of it?

65
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:10,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's watch sensations, so that they will magically dispel our unwholesome states.

66
00:07:10,164 --> 00:07:10,405
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

67
00:07:11,165 --> 00:07:13,287
[SPEAKER_00]: I am the one responsible for unwholesome states.

68
00:07:14,048 --> 00:07:21,254
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you do the walking or sitting, because it's a repetitive or still action, so it doesn't take too much of your attention to do it.

69
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:31,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you need all of your attention and focus in a sense of context and mindfulness towards recognizing the mental states that are there, that you are subjected under.

70
00:07:31,872 --> 00:07:33,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no technique for that.

71
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:42,515
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just effort based on the instruction and correct information that sends you that way and tells you that's what you need to look for.

72
00:07:46,843 --> 00:08:05,941
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, but when you are sitting enough, long enough, and observing such sensations, then, as a result of it, you can also learn a lot of your mind, how it works, about your motivations, about resistance, about everything what you are talking about.

73
00:08:05,981 --> 00:08:08,564
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you learn that because I told you that's what you need to learn.

74
00:08:10,583 --> 00:08:16,506
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to start, for example, if vipassana is truly vipassana, you wouldn't need all of this I'm telling you.

75
00:08:16,566 --> 00:08:20,589
[SPEAKER_00]: You would simply watch your sensations and arrive at the same conclusions.

76
00:08:20,609 --> 00:08:21,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't.

77
00:08:21,669 --> 00:08:29,934
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you arrive at the conclusions of, I need to see my intention, I need to understand this, I need to practice sense restraint, is because somebody told you you need to do that.

78
00:08:30,895 --> 00:08:33,396
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't need to watch your sensations for the sake of it.

79
00:08:33,416 --> 00:08:36,938
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to not forget what you have to do that you already know.

80
00:08:38,549 --> 00:08:43,995
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to sit quietly or walk up and down, do so contemplating what you need to do.

81
00:08:44,596 --> 00:08:46,838
[SPEAKER_00]: You must... and Buddha says what you need to do.

82
00:08:46,858 --> 00:08:52,124
[SPEAKER_00]: He said you should not tolerate thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, thoughts of hatred.

83
00:08:53,065 --> 00:08:56,629
[SPEAKER_00]: You should not welcome, entertain, encourage them to stay longer.

84
00:08:56,649 --> 00:08:57,891
[SPEAKER_00]: You should not act out of them.

85
00:08:58,071 --> 00:08:58,972
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you need to do.

86
00:09:00,847 --> 00:09:12,690
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just when people see... they already know this from the suttas and from the instruction, then they do this sensation watching or whatever else, that kind of results in calmness or whatever else, which is fine.

87
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:15,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Same like, you know, lay down and relax if you are tense.

88
00:09:16,771 --> 00:09:17,992
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a valid experience.

89
00:09:18,632 --> 00:09:28,595
[SPEAKER_00]: But because, you know, you were taught that there is much more to it, you end up ascribing all these values, such as, you know, seeing my motivation, intention, to that vipassana practice.

90
00:09:28,615 --> 00:09:29,635
[SPEAKER_00]: It has nothing to do with it.

91
00:09:30,915 --> 00:09:41,264
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you know that the mental states of ill-will is... are bad, is because you were told they were bad, you thought about it, you saw for yourself that they are bad, and they'll cause you suffering.

92
00:09:42,045 --> 00:09:43,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensation watching had nothing to do with it.

93
00:09:45,049 --> 00:09:55,416
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you still are doubtful about it, as I said, take the experiment of somebody who hasn't been told about the value of virtue, sensory strength, and mindfulness, and give them the sensation watching.

94
00:09:55,956 --> 00:09:58,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Will they arrive at the same values?

95
00:09:58,418 --> 00:09:59,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a chance.

96
00:09:59,299 --> 00:10:00,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in a million years.

97
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:08,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Because watching sensation is nothing other than watching bodily touches, or listening to the sounds of the ear.

98
00:10:09,105 --> 00:10:11,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, keeping the mind on the level of the senses.

99
00:10:13,038 --> 00:10:16,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on the level of the nature behind the senses that you need to understand.

100
00:10:18,061 --> 00:10:20,622
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it doesn't matter how you breathe, how you walk.

101
00:10:21,323 --> 00:10:27,246
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is to be aware of the mental states in regard to your breathing, in regard to your walking.

102
00:10:27,346 --> 00:10:28,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they wholesome?

103
00:10:28,186 --> 00:10:29,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they unwholesome?

104
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:30,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I welcoming them?

105
00:10:30,788 --> 00:10:31,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I delighting in them?

106
00:10:31,928 --> 00:10:32,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I ignoring them?

107
00:10:33,029 --> 00:10:33,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I fearing them?

108
00:10:34,230 --> 00:10:34,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

109
00:10:37,391 --> 00:10:38,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the work.

110
00:10:38,272 --> 00:10:39,392
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the true vipassana.

111
00:10:39,593 --> 00:10:40,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding.

112
00:10:44,322 --> 00:10:48,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were, if the wisdom were on the level of the senses, the Buddha would have said so.

113
00:10:48,566 --> 00:10:52,850
[SPEAKER_00]: He would say, just sit down feeling the sensations in your body and wisdom will arise.

114
00:10:53,010 --> 00:10:53,150
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

115
00:10:53,770 --> 00:10:57,354
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, sit down and understand the nature of the body within your body.

116
00:10:58,755 --> 00:11:04,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand the nature of feelings within your feelings, the nature of mind within your mind, the nature of thoughts within your thoughts.

117
00:11:05,179 --> 00:11:05,919
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you understand?

118
00:11:05,959 --> 00:11:13,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, for example, by contemplating death in regard to the body, by contemplating a subject to change in regard to the body.

119
00:11:13,242 --> 00:11:18,924
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you practice mindfulness of the body.

120
00:11:18,985 --> 00:11:22,126
[SPEAKER_00]: When you do the vipassana of the mind,

121
00:11:22,985 --> 00:11:26,708
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't watch the sensations of moment-to-moment passing awareness or whatever else.

122
00:11:27,168 --> 00:11:27,348
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

123
00:11:27,708 --> 00:11:32,092
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, you know mind affected with lust as mind affected with lust.

124
00:11:32,332 --> 00:11:33,132
[SPEAKER_00]: So you endure it.

125
00:11:33,292 --> 00:11:34,173
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't act out of it.

126
00:11:34,213 --> 00:11:35,034
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't delight in it.

127
00:11:35,074 --> 00:11:35,774
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't welcome it.

128
00:11:36,615 --> 00:11:39,617
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows mind affected with anger as mind affected with anger.

129
00:11:41,178 --> 00:11:42,479
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he watches the physical.

130
00:11:42,559 --> 00:11:42,739
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

131
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:48,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Then he doesn't lose the sight of the mind affected with anger until that anger disappears.

132
00:11:49,738 --> 00:11:59,445
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he spent the time enduring the angry mind without acting out of that angry mind, and without welcoming, delighting in it, entertaining it, prolonging it, making it worse.

133
00:12:00,266 --> 00:12:06,410
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you train that internal immovability in regard to the states of mind, feeling, and the positions of the body.

134
00:12:08,331 --> 00:12:09,632
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, that's what I mean.

135
00:12:09,672 --> 00:12:13,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely the opposite direction from the direction of sense observation.

136
00:12:14,376 --> 00:12:17,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling, touching, looking, sensing, hearing, and so on.

137
00:12:22,090 --> 00:12:27,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Would you say that this is, this is though, could be an introduction, introductory state?

138
00:12:28,292 --> 00:12:28,452
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

139
00:12:28,472 --> 00:12:30,313
[SPEAKER_02]: Start meditating.

140
00:12:30,713 --> 00:12:31,553
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

141
00:12:31,613 --> 00:12:37,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Introduction is learning about the precepts, sense restraint, value of seclusion, mindfulness.

142
00:12:37,175 --> 00:12:38,156
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your introduction.

143
00:12:38,836 --> 00:12:39,236
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

144
00:12:39,256 --> 00:12:40,016
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not what I meant.

145
00:12:40,056 --> 00:12:47,399
[SPEAKER_02]: When you sit, and you try to, well, Buddha says that the bhikkhu enters and dwells in

146
00:12:48,103 --> 00:12:49,464
[SPEAKER_02]: The first jhāna, etc.

147
00:12:49,484 --> 00:12:55,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, maybe this would be like a way, just a method, to calm the mind down, to bring it back.

148
00:12:55,829 --> 00:12:58,111
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the method to calm the mind down?

149
00:12:58,892 --> 00:13:00,473
[SPEAKER_02]: To watch the sensations.

150
00:13:01,053 --> 00:13:01,213
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

151
00:13:01,233 --> 00:13:03,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's because that's not what the mind is.

152
00:13:03,876 --> 00:13:06,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would watching the sense touches of your body?

153
00:13:07,378 --> 00:13:09,460
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, just for the first couple of minutes, maybe.

154
00:13:10,091 --> 00:13:11,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, like, yeah, sure.

155
00:13:12,354 --> 00:13:19,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you need to calm down, if you need to calm down, if you were sort of busy or agitated, yeah, calm down.

156
00:13:21,172 --> 00:13:24,474
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not how vipassana is advertised, isn't it?

157
00:13:25,115 --> 00:13:34,141
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't say, oh, this is a mere relaxation technique for which, you know, have a back massage, lay down, sleep a bit, wake up, relax, go for a walk, and then start doing the work.

158
00:13:34,542 --> 00:13:34,942
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

159
00:13:35,402 --> 00:13:37,264
[SPEAKER_00]: It's advertised as the work.

160
00:13:37,844 --> 00:13:40,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Watching sensation leads to enlightenment.

161
00:13:41,407 --> 00:13:42,548
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's simply not the case.

162
00:13:44,149 --> 00:13:46,031
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a follow-up question.

163
00:13:46,191 --> 00:14:03,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhante, how would you characterize the distinction between a. a sensation, being aware of sensations, then b. contemplation, as reflective awareness, mindfulness, as you describe it, and c. intellection, regular thinking.

164
00:14:04,506 --> 00:14:09,250
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be helpful ways of thinking about these three phenomena or processes?

165
00:14:09,963 --> 00:14:14,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sensation is basically... you need to undo all the mistaken views.

166
00:14:14,906 --> 00:14:17,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensation is almost regarded as if it's a sort of feeling.

167
00:14:18,468 --> 00:14:24,532
[SPEAKER_00]: But sensation, in reality, is just a physical sense of touch experienced internally.

168
00:14:24,572 --> 00:14:30,136
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's always measured as, oh, I'm feeling it in my knees, or my back, or my belly, and it feels like this, feels like that.

169
00:14:30,156 --> 00:14:30,756
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't feel.

170
00:14:30,776 --> 00:14:31,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You're perceiving it.

171
00:14:32,637 --> 00:14:33,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a mere sense of touch.

172
00:14:33,978 --> 00:14:37,801
[SPEAKER_00]: When you sit on a chair, you have a sensation where you're leaning on the chair.

173
00:14:39,112 --> 00:14:45,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if there is sensation of ants in my arms, for whatever reason, whether I pin the nerve or something, doesn't matter.

174
00:14:45,998 --> 00:14:50,342
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, sensations are on the level of sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

175
00:14:51,604 --> 00:14:52,905
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing more to them.

176
00:14:52,945 --> 00:14:58,030
[SPEAKER_00]: There are... there is no special property of sensations that somehow magically reveals you the truth.

177
00:15:01,873 --> 00:15:02,714
[SPEAKER_00]: What was the second one?

178
00:15:05,594 --> 00:15:08,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Reflective awareness and mindfulness, as you describe it.

179
00:15:08,962 --> 00:15:10,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the third was intellectual.

180
00:15:11,769 --> 00:15:13,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, let me start with the third one.

181
00:15:13,190 --> 00:15:14,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Third one is the intellect.

182
00:15:14,311 --> 00:15:16,353
[SPEAKER_00]: It's using your own reasoning, like this.

183
00:15:17,073 --> 00:15:23,999
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, wait a minute, these things that I read today, that say Buddhist meditation is, I don't find in the suttas.

184
00:15:24,379 --> 00:15:29,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha and the meditation that's mentioned in the suttas does not quite match the meditation you read about today.

185
00:15:30,604 --> 00:15:32,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use your reason to recognize that there is a problem there.

186
00:15:33,567 --> 00:15:34,808
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have it both ways.

187
00:15:34,848 --> 00:15:38,751
[SPEAKER_00]: If they are not saying the same thing, well, only one of them is then right.

188
00:15:40,112 --> 00:15:41,293
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't be both right.

189
00:15:43,130 --> 00:15:53,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use your intellect to discern where the quality of information, let's say like that, the accuracy of the information you're collecting about the practice.

190
00:15:55,454 --> 00:15:59,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the second one, the contemplation that reflects awareness, is exactly that.

191
00:15:59,976 --> 00:16:04,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, seeing it in practice, seeing it in your life, seeing it in your states of mind, not mere theory.

192
00:16:07,379 --> 00:16:11,240
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, really, Buddhist meditation should be Buddhist contemplation.

193
00:16:12,487 --> 00:16:13,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the idea of...

194
00:16:13,527 --> 00:16:18,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind you, meditation, even in Christianity, means that, means recollecting, you know, thinking about the God, and so on.

195
00:16:19,210 --> 00:16:26,713
[SPEAKER_00]: But today, when you say meditation, means concentrating, focusing, until I'm unaware of everything else, and I'm one-pointed, where nothing else exists.

196
00:16:27,818 --> 00:16:40,141
[SPEAKER_00]: And I said that many times, that the Buddha himself described that in the suttas as a completely wrong meditation, that he compared to, like, an animal, sort of, waiting for a cat, waiting for a mouse to pounce it on.

197
00:16:40,321 --> 00:16:43,782
[SPEAKER_00]: It's so concentrated, the whole world disappears, just sees one thing.

198
00:16:44,582 --> 00:16:48,323
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's completely the mīccha samādhi, the wrong direction of your samādhi.

199
00:16:51,583 --> 00:16:53,204
[SPEAKER_00]: So, contemplation will basically

200
00:16:55,190 --> 00:17:03,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Make you think rightly and discern that right direction I'm talking about, on the basis of rightly selected information.

201
00:17:04,494 --> 00:17:11,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you were... if you heard wrongly, you will not be able to arrive at the right conclusions, because you don't have the right instruction and right pointers.

202
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:19,461
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you describe right samādhi?

203
00:17:19,781 --> 00:17:21,822
[SPEAKER_02]: You just mentioned wrong samādhi.

204
00:17:24,853 --> 00:17:28,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, right samādhi is that, purifying your mind from the unwholesome states.

205
00:17:30,537 --> 00:17:45,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the lust is present, for example, not welcoming it, not acting out of it, not trying to get rid of it, not trying to explain it, or justify it, or avoid responsibility for it, enduring it rightly, is the right samādhi.

206
00:17:46,821 --> 00:17:50,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you learn how to endure things without acting out of it, you are practicing samādhi.

207
00:17:50,942 --> 00:17:52,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the Buddha described in the suttas.

208
00:17:53,483 --> 00:18:01,245
[SPEAKER_00]: He said samādhi is when things try to move the monk's mind, but the monk's mind doesn't move, on account of things trying to move it.

209
00:18:02,146 --> 00:18:03,366
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how he developed samādhi.

210
00:18:03,746 --> 00:18:08,007
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say samādhi is when he gets so focused on his nostrils, the whole world disappears.

211
00:18:09,968 --> 00:18:12,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's immovability in regard to things that want to move you.

212
00:18:14,023 --> 00:18:22,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, you are already practicing samādhi in a day-to-day life, whether you want it or not, because you are already forced to endure things whether you want it or not.

213
00:18:23,369 --> 00:18:27,571
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is, without the right instructions, you will not necessarily learn on account of that endurance.

214
00:18:27,912 --> 00:18:38,258
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know, as I said, how to endure things on that right level, that will result in that irreversible wisdom.

215
00:18:38,278 --> 00:18:41,360
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I thought the fourth jhāna was synonymous with right

216
00:18:42,893 --> 00:18:45,331
[SPEAKER_00]: No, the first jhāna is already synonymous with right samādhi.

217
00:18:49,343 --> 00:18:53,546
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, what is the... First of all, that right concentration means right samādhi.

218
00:18:53,826 --> 00:18:56,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just that whoever translated that chose the word concentration.

219
00:18:56,968 --> 00:18:58,469
[SPEAKER_00]: We choose composure.

220
00:18:58,609 --> 00:19:01,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, right composure is the first jhāna.

221
00:19:02,191 --> 00:19:06,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, the first jhāna is the measure of your correct samādhi.

222
00:19:06,614 --> 00:19:14,639
[SPEAKER_00]: But there are, you know, quite a bit of development before the first jhāna that goes in the direction of right samādhi, but it doesn't quite constitute the first jhāna yet.

223
00:19:15,786 --> 00:19:19,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for Sotāpanna, he doesn't need to have jhāna to get the right view.

224
00:19:20,567 --> 00:19:24,489
[SPEAKER_00]: But he does need to grasp the signs of his mind, as we spoke before.

225
00:19:25,649 --> 00:19:29,470
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a necessary prerequisite for developing the right view.

226
00:19:30,050 --> 00:19:32,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no right samādhi without the right view.

227
00:19:33,491 --> 00:19:39,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, gaining the right view means Sotāpanna already has a form of right samādhi, although he's not quite on the level of jhāna yet.

228
00:19:42,494 --> 00:19:53,460
We often talk about this activity of preparing to some situations.

229
00:19:53,560 --> 00:20:09,208
[SPEAKER_04]: But sometimes, as I observe my life and my inner processes and so on, happening at the moment, often

230
00:20:09,725 --> 00:20:22,757
[SPEAKER_04]: You are surprised by the complexity and by the magnitude of some emotions that appear and that you are not aware that they will appear.

231
00:20:23,838 --> 00:20:25,479
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can't prepare to them.

232
00:20:27,141 --> 00:20:34,768
[SPEAKER_04]: And I can observe at this particular case, when some

233
00:20:36,723 --> 00:20:39,765
[SPEAKER_04]: Life or some processes are coming to an end.

234
00:20:41,226 --> 00:20:51,072
[SPEAKER_04]: And as close as you are to an end, there comes, in my case, a big, big, big bag of guilt.

235
00:20:53,073 --> 00:21:04,801
[SPEAKER_04]: And how can you prepare and how do you deal with this big magnitude of emotions, when you feel that you have missed before and now

236
00:21:05,676 --> 00:21:12,220
[SPEAKER_00]: You are only there with a big guilt and this is... you have to deal with... Well, that's... that's the thing.

237
00:21:12,260 --> 00:21:18,063
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to start dealing with smaller emotions of a smaller magnitude before they get that big.

238
00:21:19,264 --> 00:21:21,065
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't... it doesn't get that big on its own.

239
00:21:21,365 --> 00:21:26,548
[SPEAKER_00]: It gets that big because you've been ignoring all the small signs and tokens that are pointing that it's gonna get that big.

240
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:36,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have been ignoring and now you're facing a big guilt, well, the first thing you have to do is not act out of it and endure it until it ceases.

241
00:21:37,505 --> 00:21:38,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, learn your lesson from it.

242
00:21:39,146 --> 00:21:43,369
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense that, remember now how big that guilt was and how unpleasant.

243
00:21:43,409 --> 00:21:46,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time, don't wait that long.

244
00:21:46,631 --> 00:21:48,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't let it get that much out of hand.

245
00:21:50,573 --> 00:21:50,793
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

246
00:21:52,878 --> 00:22:10,147
[SPEAKER_04]: Because sometimes, if you ignore and you don't contemplate it, it can appear that you reject this process of handling the situation and it can get you to really deep and dark places.

247
00:22:10,828 --> 00:22:12,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, because you turn a blind eye to it.

248
00:22:13,529 --> 00:22:14,409
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you need to do.

249
00:22:14,429 --> 00:22:17,031
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to, like, figure out guilt before it comes.

250
00:22:17,071 --> 00:22:20,813
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to recognize that it always remains as a possibility.

251
00:22:22,120 --> 00:22:27,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't ignore that possibility of eventual discomfort, pain and resistance to it.

252
00:22:27,785 --> 00:22:29,887
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you learn how to not resist to that.

253
00:22:30,147 --> 00:22:32,229
[SPEAKER_00]: How to endure it and not act out of it.

254
00:22:32,970 --> 00:22:36,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how it will cease to pressure you.

255
00:22:36,694 --> 00:22:39,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also how you will not lose perspective when guilt arises.

256
00:22:40,117 --> 00:22:42,979
[SPEAKER_00]: You will see whether it's a rational guilt or irrational guilt.

257
00:22:43,580 --> 00:22:46,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a guilt rooted in something that you genuinely did wrong,

258
00:22:47,767 --> 00:22:52,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Or not rooted in anything you did wrong, but just your own mind winding you up and attacking you.

259
00:22:57,014 --> 00:23:05,039
[SPEAKER_04]: But what happens when you're faced with situations where you can't change the...

260
00:23:05,920 --> 00:23:09,062
I mean, if some people are gone, how can you change the...

261
00:23:11,178 --> 00:23:12,879
[SPEAKER_04]: But you have done wrong in the past.

262
00:23:13,119 --> 00:23:13,579
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

263
00:23:14,319 --> 00:23:16,340
[SPEAKER_00]: You can stop resisting the guilt here and now.

264
00:23:16,900 --> 00:23:18,801
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you free yourself from those actions.

265
00:23:19,361 --> 00:23:22,623
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how Angulimala freed himself from all the murders he committed.

266
00:23:23,823 --> 00:23:25,184
[SPEAKER_00]: He became an arahant here and now.

267
00:23:28,805 --> 00:23:29,525
[SPEAKER_00]: He couldn't change.

268
00:23:29,565 --> 00:23:30,966
[SPEAKER_00]: He couldn't undo his actions.

269
00:23:32,492 --> 00:23:41,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But he did finally resolve the root of all action, which is craving in regard to feeling, and because of that, none of those actions actually followed him any further.

270
00:23:42,859 --> 00:23:50,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason you would want to, even theoretically, try and change the past, is because you don't want to endure the present guilt.

271
00:23:52,725 --> 00:23:56,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, stop resisting the present guilt and past will cease to be a problem.

272
00:23:59,708 --> 00:24:03,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Could Bhante perhaps share a few more words on how to deal with guilt?

273
00:24:03,710 --> 00:24:09,192
[SPEAKER_01]: How to accept the previous mistakes, paths not taken, wrong paths, etc.?

274
00:24:09,612 --> 00:24:12,213
[SPEAKER_01]: How to stop resisting guilt, if there's time?

275
00:24:12,713 --> 00:24:12,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

276
00:24:13,653 --> 00:24:18,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, you would... all of those things you would want to do, because you don't want to feel guilt.

277
00:24:18,915 --> 00:24:25,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would... how to accept past mistakes, so I don't feel bad today, on account of past mistakes.

278
00:24:26,357 --> 00:24:32,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, your acceptance of past mistakes is fueling your guilt, because of which you want to accept the past mistakes.

279
00:24:33,442 --> 00:24:34,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a vicious circle.

280
00:24:34,443 --> 00:24:39,506
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to do it is to stop... to endure the discomfort first.

281
00:24:39,686 --> 00:24:40,587
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to endure it.

282
00:24:41,767 --> 00:24:42,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, stop resisting it.

283
00:24:42,988 --> 00:24:44,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to accept the past.

284
00:24:45,050 --> 00:24:52,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to come to terms with it, because all of that is rooted in you still resisting the presently-endured

285
00:24:57,275 --> 00:25:03,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you first stop doing everything or anything on account of it and endure it, then you'll see where to go.

286
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:09,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you'll see that within that non-resistance, you can get to look closer at that guilt which is there.

287
00:25:10,385 --> 00:25:12,727
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason it hurts is because you resist it.

288
00:25:15,289 --> 00:25:17,470
[SPEAKER_00]: In itself, it doesn't.

289
00:25:17,510 --> 00:25:18,411
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't cause you pain.

290
00:25:19,452 --> 00:25:26,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a hot burning embers don't hurt unless, you know, you reach out with your hand to grab them.

291
00:25:28,150 --> 00:25:28,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

292
00:25:30,351 --> 00:25:31,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop reaching out.

293
00:25:31,652 --> 00:25:32,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you stop reaching out?

294
00:25:32,933 --> 00:25:37,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by enduring the pain first, to see what causes it directly.

295
00:25:37,856 --> 00:25:47,322
[SPEAKER_00]: The present pain is not caused by things you did in the past, things done to you in the past, things you missed in the past, opportunities you... whatever else you didn't seize and so on.

296
00:25:48,182 --> 00:25:52,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Present pain is caused by present resistance to a present feeling.

297
00:25:52,863 --> 00:25:53,223
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

298
00:25:54,004 --> 00:25:55,104
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first noble truth.

299
00:25:55,784 --> 00:25:57,645
[SPEAKER_00]: The root of suffering is not the past.

300
00:25:57,745 --> 00:25:58,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the guilt.

301
00:25:58,605 --> 00:26:00,725
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not who said what said when they said it.

302
00:26:01,466 --> 00:26:04,066
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically taṇha in regard to what you feel.

303
00:26:04,546 --> 00:26:05,467
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you suffer.

304
00:26:05,827 --> 00:26:06,347
[SPEAKER_00]: There and then.

305
00:26:09,848 --> 00:26:14,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you really want to take it in a broader sense, it doesn't really matter what type of feeling there is.

306
00:26:15,512 --> 00:26:24,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a guilt, whether it's a pleasant feeling, the point is, you need to learn how, here and now, not to resist it and not to indulge it.

307
00:26:25,678 --> 00:26:29,040
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how not to lose perspective in regard to what you feel here and now.

308
00:26:29,180 --> 00:26:29,581
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all.

309
00:26:30,290 --> 00:26:35,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Really, just don't forget about yourself in regard to how you're feeling in this present moment.

310
00:26:36,612 --> 00:26:40,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you sustain that attitude, that's basically the correct practice of mindfulness.

311
00:26:41,753 --> 00:26:47,755
[SPEAKER_00]: A perspective being deeply rooted in your gratuitous resistance to a presently enduring feeling.

312
00:26:48,595 --> 00:26:50,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Because feeling is there whether you resist it or not.

313
00:26:53,336 --> 00:26:55,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So until you stop resisting it, it will bother you.

314
00:26:59,601 --> 00:27:06,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I have a somewhat related question, now that I've been listening to this advice.

315
00:27:07,026 --> 00:27:11,209
[SPEAKER_01]: It was basically how to improve tolerance to pain.

316
00:27:11,870 --> 00:27:15,773
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, certain pains I have no problems with.

317
00:27:15,813 --> 00:27:18,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if my finger hurts, it's not a big deal.

318
00:27:19,035 --> 00:27:23,638
[SPEAKER_01]: But, for example, pain in the head.

319
00:27:24,679 --> 00:27:27,721
[SPEAKER_01]: I find myself quickly grabbing the pills, because it

320
00:27:29,208 --> 00:27:30,969
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very difficult for me to think clearly.

321
00:27:31,790 --> 00:27:38,775
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, there are pains in the body where your mind remains intact, and there are pains that make it harder to think.

322
00:27:38,855 --> 00:27:45,040
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm thinking that this is also probably the reason why my tolerance to pain is so low.

323
00:27:45,500 --> 00:27:54,787
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's also very hard to decide and insist upon it when you're not even really able to think, when you're in the

324
00:27:56,737 --> 00:27:59,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so you have to decide before that pain.

325
00:27:59,118 --> 00:28:01,398
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to anticipate it and then decide what to do.

326
00:28:02,778 --> 00:28:10,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this case, you know, if it's like a serious, life-threatening pain that might compromise, you know, your ability in long term, then you address it.

327
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:24,303
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's just an uncomfortable headache that will prevent you to think for a while and kind of, you know, make you numb, painful and confused, well, really, the reason you're taking the medicine there is not because of the pain in the head, it's because of the fear.

328
00:28:25,802 --> 00:28:30,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Of losing that clarity and levels of management and so on.

329
00:28:31,967 --> 00:28:34,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, it's a psychological cause.

330
00:28:34,769 --> 00:28:43,376
[SPEAKER_00]: You're afraid of the pain that might sort of interfere with your operation and that scares you and that's why you take the pill.

331
00:28:44,036 --> 00:28:51,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you... if it's not, as I said, if it's just a headache, life-threatening pain, not life-threatening pain, you could just decide beforehand to not do it and endure it.

332
00:28:52,852 --> 00:28:55,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And experience that discomfort and confusion and everything else that comes with it.

333
00:28:56,598 --> 00:28:57,921
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no other magical way.

334
00:28:57,941 --> 00:29:01,206
[SPEAKER_00]: What can I do so I don't have to go through it, but get the results of it?

335
00:29:02,148 --> 00:29:02,308
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

336
00:29:02,348 --> 00:29:04,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Tolerance is developed through tolerance.

337
00:29:11,497 --> 00:29:21,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's really... the important part is to recognize that these feelings... enduring of the feelings will purify you only if you're enduring them on the right level.

338
00:29:22,819 --> 00:29:29,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're enduring, like, random feelings and random discomforts of the physical kind, that will not do anything necessarily to you.

339
00:29:29,821 --> 00:29:41,143
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said, you know, self-mortification and asceticism is misguided, because people assume that the enduring physical discomforts will somehow lead them, you know, to purification and everything else.

340
00:29:42,083 --> 00:29:54,927
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you endure the feelings on the right level, which is that mental level, see, you have to endure now the pressure to act out of fear in regard to your headache that will impede you and complicate your day.

341
00:29:56,347 --> 00:30:06,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you endure non-action on the level of that fear, that endurance of that feeling will actually contribute towards the purification, will contribute to a strengthening of the mind.

342
00:30:07,363 --> 00:30:12,126
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just enduring the pain in your leg for the sake of a pain in your leg, that's equally pointless.

343
00:30:18,450 --> 00:30:23,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, as long as you get to learn something from it, then it's valuable?

344
00:30:23,693 --> 00:30:35,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, as long as you see where it is rooted, whether it's rooted in, sort of, what state of mind is the root of it, and then you endure it on the level of the state of mind.

345
00:30:37,313 --> 00:30:45,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you start seeing it there, then, see, you realize fear of the headache is the problem, not the headache in itself.

346
00:30:47,460 --> 00:30:52,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you're liable to fear and it can be caused then by other things, not just the headache.

347
00:30:53,023 --> 00:31:03,110
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you start enduring that feeling on that level, not acting out of it, not resisting it, you are actually preparing your mind for any type of discomfort and

348
00:31:04,005 --> 00:31:08,909
[SPEAKER_00]: unpleasant confusion and so on, that might arise on account of various circumstances for as long as you live.

349
00:31:15,234 --> 00:31:21,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So far, I haven't realized that this was actually... there was fear behind it, but it makes sense.

350
00:31:21,138 --> 00:31:21,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you for this one.

351
00:31:29,790 --> 00:31:34,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear or resistance through having to... psychological resistance through having to go through that ordeal?

352
